From: KRdp displayless experiment <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] Server: never join a session thread from the Qt main thread

forceTerminateConnection() erased the session from Server::Private::sessions,
which runs ~RdpConnection inline. That destructor joins the krdp_session
worker, and the worker's exit path is run() -> onClose() -> VideoStream::close(),
which marshals onto the main thread. Calling it from a QTimer slot deadlocks
the two against each other.

The consequence is worse than a lost session: an event loop stuck that way
stops calling accept(), so the listening socket keeps queueing clients nobody
answers. Connections hang rather than fail, and the service has to be restarted
out of band. Seen in the field as a main thread parked in futex_do_wait with
five connections backed up in the listen queue, preceded by
"pw_stream_set_active called from wrong context" as the dying worker reached
into PipeWire from the wrong context.

Close the peer and let the connection die on its own schedule. peer->Close() is
asynchronous and safe from any thread; the Closed notification that follows
erases the session from the main thread with the worker already finished, where
the join costs nothing. A peer that ignores the close is quarantined rather than
joined, and destroyed later once it reports Closed. An orphaned connection costs
a thread; a blocking join costs the whole server.

diff --git a/src/Server.cpp b/src/Server.cpp
index caecd96..8cefdeb 100644
--- a/src/Server.cpp
+++ b/src/Server.cpp
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 #include <vector>
 
 #include <QCoreApplication>
+#include <QTimer>
 
 #include <freerdp/channels/channels.h>
 #include <freerdp/freerdp.h>
@@ -18,10 +19,21 @@
 
 using namespace KRdp;
 
+/// How long a force-terminated peer is given to close before it is quarantined.
+static constexpr int ForceCloseGraceMs = 5000;
+
+/// How often a quarantined connection is re-checked for having finished.
+static constexpr int QuarantineReapMs = 2000;
+
 class KRDP_NO_EXPORT Server::Private
 {
 public:
     std::vector<std::unique_ptr<RdpConnection>> sessions;
+
+    /// Connections that ignored peer->Close(). Held here instead of being joined;
+    /// see Server::forceTerminateConnection.
+    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<RdpConnection>> quarantine;
+
     rdp_settings *settings = nullptr;
 
     QHostAddress address = QHostAddress::LocalHost;
@@ -188,8 +200,51 @@ void Server::forceTerminateConnection(RdpConnection *connection)
     }
 
     qCWarning(KRDP) << "Force-terminating stuck RDP connection" << connection;
+
+    // Only ask the peer to close, and do not destroy the connection here.
+    // ~RdpConnection joins the krdp_session worker, and that worker's exit path
+    // is run() -> onClose() -> VideoStream::close(), which marshals onto the
+    // main thread. Joining from the main thread deadlocks the two against each
+    // other, and an event loop stuck that way stops calling accept(): later
+    // connections then queue on the listening socket unanswered, so the whole
+    // server has to be restarted out of band.
+    //
+    // peer->Close() is asynchronous and safe from any thread. It breaks the
+    // worker out of WaitForMultipleObjects, and the Closed notification that
+    // follows erases the session from the main thread with the worker already
+    // finished, where the join costs nothing.
     (*itr)->close();
-    d->sessions.erase(itr);
+
+    QTimer::singleShot(ForceCloseGraceMs, this, [this, connection] {
+        const auto itr = std::find_if(d->sessions.begin(), d->sessions.end(), [connection](const auto &session) {
+            return session.get() == connection;
+        });
+        if (itr == d->sessions.end()) {
+            return; // it closed on its own, and the Closed path already erased it
+        }
+
+        qCWarning(KRDP) << "RDP connection ignored close; quarantining rather than joining" << connection;
+        d->quarantine.push_back(std::move(*itr));
+        d->sessions.erase(itr);
+        reapQuarantine();
+    });
+}
+
+void Server::reapQuarantine()
+{
+    // Destroy only what has finished: for a connection already in Closed the
+    // join in ~RdpConnection returns immediately. One still running is left
+    // alone and retried. An orphaned connection costs a thread; a blocking join
+    // costs the whole server.
+    std::erase_if(d->quarantine, [](const std::unique_ptr<RdpConnection> &session) {
+        return session->state() == RdpConnection::State::Closed;
+    });
+
+    if (!d->quarantine.empty()) {
+        QTimer::singleShot(QuarantineReapMs, this, [this] {
+            reapQuarantine();
+        });
+    }
 }
 
 rdp_settings *Server::rdpSettings() const
diff --git a/src/Server.h b/src/Server.h
index 97c3c6f..aa0b31e 100644
--- a/src/Server.h
+++ b/src/Server.h
@@ -116,8 +116,12 @@ public:
     Q_SIGNAL void newConnectionCreated(RdpConnection *connection);
 
     /**
-     * Destroy a stuck connection through the server's ownership collection.
-     * This closes the FreeRDP peer before joining its worker thread.
+     * Ask a stuck connection to close, without waiting for it.
+     *
+     * Never joins the session worker. RdpConnection's destructor does, and that
+     * worker needs the main thread to finish, so joining from here deadlocks the
+     * event loop and stops the server accepting connections. A peer that ignores
+     * the close is quarantined and destroyed later, once it reports Closed.
      */
     void forceTerminateConnection(RdpConnection *connection);
 
@@ -131,6 +135,9 @@ private:
     friend class RdpConnection;
     rdp_settings *rdpSettings() const;
 
+    /// Destroy quarantined connections that have finished, and re-arm if any remain.
+    void reapQuarantine();
+
     class Private;
     const std::unique_ptr<Private> d;
 };
